# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on this machine rewrites checked-out files to CRLF. That is
# fine for editing and wrong for anything whose *content* is compared as a
# string across machines.
#
# Terraform is the concrete case: a heredoc in an `output` block becomes a value
# stored in state. Write the file with LF, store LF in state, then check it out
# as CRLF, and every subsequent plan reports the output as changed -- each line
# removed and re-added with identical text. Worse, a Linux CI runner checks out
# LF while a Windows laptop has CRLF, so the two never agree on a plan even
# though they share the same state.
#
# Forcing LF makes the bytes identical everywhere.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.tf     text eol=lf
*.tfvars text eol=lf
*.hcl    text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf

# Shell scripts that run on a Linux box cannot have CRLF at all -- the shebang
# becomes "#!/bin/sh\r", and the error is "no such file or directory" naming an
# interpreter that plainly exists.
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
*.Caddyfile     text eol=lf

# Windows-only, and genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
